abstract: "Estuaries provide critical habitat for over 80% of BC\u2019s coastal fish\
  \ and wildlife, including Pacific salmon, marine mammals, and migratory birds. The\
  \ complex structure and aquatic vegetation of the estuary also serve to filter sediments,\
  \ sequester carbon dioxide (CO2), and stabilize shorelines from erosion. Anthropogenic\
  \ stressors from aquaculture, shoreline development, forestry, and pollution can\
  \ degrade estuarine habitats, impacting spatial coverage and species diversity.\
  \ In addition to human impacts, climate change is expected to significantly impact\
  \ estuary ecosystems through several mechanisms, including rising sea-levels, ocean\
  \ acidification, temperature and salinity changes, and changes to freshwater and\
  \ sediment inputs. Monitoring these variables through time will provide an effective\
  \ indication of ecosystem health and productivity, informing decision makers of\
  \ estuary impacts and resilience to climate change. \n\nFrom 2019 to 2026, The Nature\
  \ Trust of British Columbia (NTBC) implemented a monitoring project to assess marsh\
  \ resilience to sea-level rise. As a part of the monitoring program, NTBC partnered\
  \ with coastal First Nations to collect detailed water quality data from 20 estuaries\
  \ on Vancouver Island, the Central Coast, and Haida Gwaii.  \n\nWater quality data\
  \ are collected using an RBR Maestro3 multi-parameter sonde - a multichannel, multi-sensor\
  \ data logging instrument designed for water column and estuary profiling. The sondes\
  \ include sensors for conductivity, temperature, and depth (CTD), dissolved oxygen\
  \ (DO), turbidity (Tu), and photosynthetically active radiation (PAR); certain instruments\
  \ also include pH (water alkalinity / acidity) sensors. Data are collected during\
  \ different weather events, tides (flood and ebb), and seasons (spring, summer,\
  \ fall, and winter) to fully understand the water quality characteristics within\
  \ the estuaries. These surveys are collected quarterly, with additional surveys\
  \ collected at some sites during some years, based on staff availability and funding.\
  \ For the Malksope estuary, water quality surveys were conducted by Ka:'yu:'k't'h'/Che:k'tles7et'h'\
  \ First Nations from December 2025 to present. \n\nWater quality surveys within\
  \ each estuary were designed to collect cast data along a salinity gradient, from\
  \ marine to fresh water. Casts were also conducted in locations that may influence\
  \ water quality in the estuary, including tributaries (freshwater streams that flow\
  \ into the estuary or the river), urban streams and runoff, and potential sources\
  \ of effluent (stormwater or wastewater, often from industrial activity)."
